WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.579 align:start position:0%
 
well joining me now for central London  is Lord Paton the former governor of  Hong Kong who oversaw the colony being  handed back by Britain to China at 22  years ago  Lord pattern thanks very much indeed for  your time this afternoon first of all  what's your reaction to those scenes  that we saw in Hong Kong last night I'm  extremely sad about them because of  course they're not at all like the  earlier demonstrations by students which  were peaceful on the whole I think it's  extremely regrettable that there was  violence yesterday and I don't think  that's acceptable and I think it  actually takes away from the really  important part of what's been happening  which you referred to earlier in the  report that hundreds of thousands of  people up to two million people have  been demonstrating peacefully and  lawfully about their rights as citizens  which they're anxious to retain I think  the problem when violence creeps in as  it plainly has for whatever reason is  that it takes away from the from the  credibility of that campaign by hundreds  of thousands of people I very much hope  the government will among other things  have a public inquiry into the  demonstrations that have taken place  over recent weeks and to the way they've  been policed and include in that what  happened yesterday because everybody  should be keen to know what exactly  happened why the police behaved as they  did above all who was actually  responsible for the violence well yes  and correct me if I'm wrong but I  understand that you have a bit some  sympathy with one of the issues at the  heart of these protests this  controversial  extradition bill which was was  introduced some concerns that that might  be used to send political dissidents to  China given that you have some sympathy  with the protesters do you worry then  that their argument has now been  undermined well I worry that the point  that's been made by all those peaceful  demonstrators don't forget the last but  one of those demonstrations with about 2  million people was led by people in  wheelchairs families lawyers business  people manual workers the whole  community was taking part because for  them  the extradition law that was proposed  demolished the  a wall between the rule of law in Hong  Kong and what passes for the law in  mainland China now that's the biggest  issue the fact that it's probably the  case that a few students behaved so  stupidly and were violent even though  you can I suppose understand their  frustrations even if you don't agree  with the way they express them we  shouldn't allow that violence to take  away from the biggest issue but which is  the way in which Hong Kong is gradually  having the noose tightened around it by  mainland China well yes and what do you  think mainland China will do now we've  heard that serious illegal actions have  they been described by by China and they  talked about the protesters trampling on  the rule of law so what do you think  their next step could be well I think  it's very odd to hear Chinese officials  talking about trampling on the rule of  law when they incarcerated about a  million people in Xinjiang I hope that  the Chinese authorities the Hong Kong  Macao Affairs Office which looks after  Hong Kong at a distance the Foreign  Ministry will be rather careful about  what they say and I hope that they will  stop suggesting that the joint  declaration doesn't have any relevance  after 1997 the joint declaration was a  treaty signed by Britain and signed by  China in good faith and it goes on until  2040 47 it gives us every right to talk  about what's happening in Hong Kong to  discuss it with China to discuss it with  the Hong Kong authorities that is our  right under international law and if  China says forget about it what they're  actually saying is you can't trust us to  keep our word well yes and that's a  joint declaration one country two  systems' was agreed as you said in 1997  when you left as Hong Kong governor what  is your feeling about that do you feel  that it's being seriously undermined by  China at the moment I think it is being  at the moment I think to be fair to  China and before this president before  President Xi Jinping the Chinese  authorities had been pretty good about  Hong Kong not everything was perfect  they choked back on the development of  democracy in Hong Kong but by and large  I think Hong Kong you could still regard  as operating with a high degree of  autonomy and  the rule of law with all the freedoms  you'd associate with an plural and open  society I think that has been brought  into question since 2014 when to be  frank I think the Chinese government the  regime in Beijing were unsettled by the  democracy demonstrations which were  peaceful unlike what happened last night  with a few people who I think behaved  really foolishly in smashing through  into the Legislative Council building  and allegedly because they didn't think  that it really represented them trashing  it that was not a sensible way to behave  and I'm sure that more sensible members  of the community who were taking part in  those demonstrations those peaceful  demonstrations in the hundred thousand  over the last few weeks will make that  plain to them China is called this a  domestic issue and says that Britain  shouldn't interfere you were saying that  Britain should have the right given the  fact that it was a joint declaration  between Britain and China that  determines the future structure for Hong  Kong so what would you want Britain to  do now well not only should Britain  intervene should have the right but it  does have the right and I'm pleased that  Jeremy Hunt the Foreign Secretary said  what he did say and this is an  international treaty China is obliged to  it until 1997 we were obliged to honor  our commitments to the people of Hong  Kong and explain what we were doing to  China after 1997 until 2047 China is  obliged to explain to the people of Hong  Kong and to us how it's keeping its  commitments to the citizens of Hong Kong  and it can't simply walk away from that  unless it wants the rest of the world to  believe that it can't be trusted to keep  its word well trying to be concerned  about contagion of some kind elsewhere  in the country well the Chinese keep on  telling us that the China dream is  something that everybody can share but  they seem jolly unsettled when anybody  else has a views of their own or wants  to have free speech or wants to look up  things on the internet they're very  concerned that people shouldn't know  what happened in Tiananmen Square in  1989 so they have to get their I have to  get their story right the truth of the  matter is the China is an old-fashioned  Leninist auteur  autocracy China has done extremely well  economic economically over the last 30  or 40 years that doesn't mean that its  system of government is one which is  exportable and it certainly doesn't mean  that it can trash the freedoms of Hong  Kong and Hong Kong's leader Carrie Lam  suspended this extradition bill that  we've referred to protesters want it  scrapped completely they want her to  stand down do you think she should go  well I'm not going to get into that  argument that's a question of her partly  although she's obviously there because  China wants her there what is absolutely  plain I think is first of all all she's  got to do is say I'm not just suspending  the bill I'm not gonna reintroduce it  the bill is dead secondly I think she  should as I said earlier she should have  a public inquiry into the whole question  of the policing of demonstrations  including what happened yesterday I  think that would if it was transparent  and open and conducted by a senior  independent well-regarded local figure I  think that would help to remove some of  the anxieties and concerns people have  on both sides both about the way that  peaceful demonstrations are policed and  about the way that some demonstrations  haven't been fully peaceful and should  be made Polly peaceful in the future 22  years it's you set sail from Hong Kong  completing your stand as the last  Governor there how do you feel seeing  those pictures that we saw yesterday and  seeing the protests over the last few  weeks how does it feel for you well I  feel very bad about the protests that  happened yesterday but when I look at  what's happened over the last few weeks  I feel a great sense of shame not not  because of anything happening in Hong  Kong I think people have behaved  extraordinarily bravely and decently but  they behaved seriously and I can't help  but compare that with what's happening  in British politics I think we have some  lessons to learn from those hundreds of  thousands of people who've behaved so  bravely and so sensibly in order to  stand up for the balance between  political and economic freedom in Hong  Kong  I wish the state of our debate in this  country was rather more serious do you  want to elaborate on that a little bit  further  well what I would I'd like to say is  here we are in a debate about the future  of our country which is full of  mendacity and delusion and every day  there come another set of delusions at  present we seem to be thinking that  there are forests as somebody said of  money bearing trees out there waiting to  shower their gifts on the rather limited  number of members of the Conservative  Party I think what we're seeing is  serious government departing from  traditional conservative values and  forgetting about the national interest  and I think that's deeply saddened but  obviously the main thing I'm concerned  about at the moment is the future of  Hong Kong I hope that here in Britain we  can behave bit more sensibly that's  rather a limited amount that I can do  about that

